    const starterQuestion={id:"jee-main-2026-april-2-set-1-q-unassigned",text:"The diameter of a wire measured by a screw gauge of least count 0.001 cm is 0.08 cm. The length measured by a scale of least count 0.1 cm is 150 cm. When a weight of 100 N is applied to the wire, the extension in length is 0.5 cm, measured by a micrometer of least count 0.001 cm. The error in the measured Young's modulus is α × 10⁹ N/m². The value of α is _____. (Ignore the contribution of the load to Young's modulus error calculation)",options:"(1) 1.3\n(2) 1.65\n(3) 0.13\n(4) 0.25",source:"User-provided question and solution screenshots",questionNumber:"",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2nd",session:"",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"Moderate",unit:1,chapter:"Units and measurements",topic:"Errors in measurements",secondary:["Young's modulus","propagation of errors","least count"],sourceAnswer:"Y = Fℓ/(AΔℓ). Since A = πd²/4, ΔY/Y = ΔF/F + Δℓ/ℓ + 2Δd/d + Δ(Δℓ)/Δℓ. The question says to ignore load error, so ΔF = 0. Fractional error = 0.1/150 + 2×0.001/0.08 + 0.001/0.5 = 0.0277. Y = [100×1.5] / [(π/4)(0.08/100)² × (0.5×10⁻²)] ≈ 5.97×10¹⁰ N/m². Therefore ΔY = 0.0277×5.97×10¹⁰ ≈ 1.65×10⁹ N/m².",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"Checked independently: relative uncertainty = 0.1/150 + 2(0.001/0.08) + 0.001/0.5 ≈ 0.02767. Converting lengths to SI gives Y ≈ 5.97×10¹⁰ N/m², so ΔY ≈ 1.65×10⁹ N/m². Thus α = 1.65, option (2).",status:"Verified",createdAt:1791998640554,simulation:{engine:"youngs-modulus-error",version:1,parameters:{forceN:100,lengthCm:150,lengthLeastCountCm:0.1,diameterCm:0.08,diameterLeastCountCm:0.001,extensionCm:0.5,extensionLeastCountCm:0.001,ignoreForceError:true}}};
    const velocityFieldQuestion={id:"jee-main-q28-velocity-field-acceleration-v2",text:"The velocity of a particle is given as v = -x i + 2y j - z k m/s. The magnitude of acceleration at point (1, 2, 4) is ____ m/s².",options:"(1) √6\n(2) 9\n(3) √33\n(4) 0",source:"User-provided implementation prompt",questionNumber:"28",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2 April",session:"1",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"",unit:2,chapter:"Motion in a plane",topic:"Velocity field and acceleration",secondary:["Velocity field","Material derivative"],sourceAnswer:"Option (2): 9 m/s². For this steady field, the material derivative gives a = (v·∇)v = (x, 4y, z); at (1,2,4), a = (1,8,4) m/s² and |a| = 9 m/s².",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"Using the material derivative, a = ∂v/∂t + (v·∇)v. There is no explicit time dependence. With v = (-x,2y,-z), the components are ax = -dx/dt = x, ay = 2dy/dt = 4y, and az = -dz/dt = z. Thus at (1,2,4), a = (1,8,4) m/s² and |a| = √(1²+8²+4²) = 9 m/s², option (2).",status:"In review",createdAt:Date.now(),simulation:{engine:"velocity-field-acceleration",version:1,parameters:{x:1,y:2,z:4}}};
    const positionDependentQuestion={id:"jee-main-2026-april-2-set-1-q29-position-dependent-motion",text:"The position of an object having mass 0.1 kg as a function of time t is given as r = (10t² i + 5t³ j) m. At t = 1 s, which of the following statements are correct? A. The linear momentum p = (2 i + 1.5 j) kg m/s. B. The force acting on the object F = (2 i + 3 j) N. C. The angular momentum of the object about its origin L = 15 k J s. D. The torque acting on the object about its origin τ = 20 k N m. Choose the correct answer.",options:"1. A, B and C only\n2. B, C and D only\n3. A, C and D only\n4. A, B and D only",source:"User-provided Question 29 implementation prompt",questionNumber:"29",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2nd",session:"",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"",unit:3,chapter:"Laws of motion",topic:"Position-dependent motion: momentum, force, angular momentum and torque",secondary:["Position function","Linear momentum","Force","Angular momentum","Torque"],sourceAnswer:"Printed answer: option (4). At t=1 s: r=(10,5) m, v=(20,15) m/s, a=(20,30) m/s², p=(2,1.5) kg m/s, F=(2,3) N, Lz=10(1.5)-5(2)=5 J s, and τz=10(3)-5(2)=20 N m. Statements A, B and D only are correct.",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"Differentiate r=(10t²,5t³) to obtain v=(20t,15t²) and a=(20,30t). At t=1 s and m=0.1 kg, p=mv=(2,1.5) kg m/s and F=ma=(2,3) N. Angular momentum about the origin has Lz=xpy-ypx=10(1.5)-5(2)=5 J s, so statement C is false. Torque has τz=xFy-yFx=10(3)-5(2)=20 N m, so D is true. Therefore option (4), A, B and D only.",status:"In review",createdAt:Date.now(),simulation:{engine:"position-dependent-motion",version:1,parameters:{massKg:0.1,timeSeconds:1}}};
    const electricFieldEnergyDensityQuestion={id:"jee-main-q26-electric-field-energy-density",text:"The dimensional formula of (1/2) ε₀E², where ε₀ is the permittivity of vacuum and E is the electric field, is MᵃLᵇTᶜ. The value of 2a − b + c is _____.",options:"1. 0\n2. 1\n3. -1\n4. 2",source:"User-provided Question 26 implementation prompt",questionNumber:"26",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2nd",session:"",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"",unit:1,chapter:"Dimensions and dimensional analysis",topic:"Dimensional analysis of electric-field energy density",secondary:["Energy density","Dimensional formula","Electric field","Vacuum permittivity"],sourceAnswer:"Printed answer: option (2).",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"The expression u = (1/2) ε₀E² is electric-field energy density. Energy per volume has dimensions [u] = (ML²T⁻²)/(L³) = ML⁻¹T⁻². The factor 1/2 is dimensionless. Comparing with MᵃLᵇTᶜ gives a=1, b=−1, c=−2. Thus 2a−b+c = 2(1)−(−1)+(−2)=1, so the answer is option (2). Independently, [ε₀]=M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴I² and [E]=MLT⁻³I⁻¹, so [ε₀E²]=ML⁻¹T⁻² with I cancelling.",status:"In review",createdAt:Date.now(),simulation:{engine:"electric-field-energy-density",version:1,parameters:{}}};
    const keplerOrbitalPeriodQuestion={id:"jee-main-q30-kepler-orbital-period",text:"A planet P1 is moving around a star of mass 2M in an orbit of radius R. Another planet P2 is moving around another star of mass 4M in an orbit of radius 2R. The ratio of time periods of revolution of P2 and P1 is _____.",options:"(1) 1/2\n(2) 2\n(3) 4\n(4) 1/4",source:"User-provided Question 30 implementation prompt",questionNumber:"30",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2nd",session:"",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"",unit:6,chapter:"Gravitation",topic:"Kepler's Third Law: Comparing Orbital Time Periods",secondary:["Kepler's laws","Circular orbits","Orbital period"],sourceAnswer:"Printed answer: option (2).",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"Kepler's third law gives T proportional to r^(3/2)/sqrt(Ms). T2/T1=(r2/r1)^(3/2)*sqrt(Ms1/Ms2)=2^(3/2)*sqrt(2M/4M)=2. Therefore option (2).",status:"In review",createdAt:Date.now(),simulation:{engine:"kepler-orbital-period",version:1,parameters:{mass1:2,radius1:1,mass2:4,radius2:2}}};
    const angularKinematicsQuestion={id:"jee-main-2026-april-2-set-1-q31-angular-kinematics",text:"A particle is rotating in a circular path and at any instant its motion can be described as \u03B8 = 5t^4/40 \u2212 t^3/3. The angular acceleration of the particle after 10 seconds is ______ rad/s\u00B2.",options:"(1) 150\n(2) 120\n(3) 130\n(4) 170",source:"User-provided Question 31 implementation prompt",questionNumber:"31",exam:"JEE Main",year:"2026",month:"April",date:"2nd",session:"",shift:"",paper:"",set:"1",difficulty:"",unit:5,chapter:"Rotational Motion",topic:"Angular position, velocity and acceleration",secondary:["Angular position","Angular velocity","Angular acceleration"],sourceAnswer:"Printed answer: option (3).",aiExplanation:"",verifiedSolution:"theta(t)=t^4/8-t^3/3, omega(t)=t^3/2-t^2, and alpha(t)=domega/dt=3t^2/2-2t. Thus alpha(10)=150-20=130 rad/s^2, option (3). Independently, theta(10)=1250-1000/3=2750/3 rad and omega(10)=400 rad/s.",status:"In review",createdAt:Date.now(),simulation:{engine:"angular-kinematics",version:1,parameters:{timeSeconds:0}}};
